 nt Situation in Maine - August 2026\nServices\nJobs & Training\nUnemployme
 nt Insurance\nDisability Services\nWorkplace Safety\nLabor Laws\nLabor Sta
 tistics\nWorkforce Development\nThe Employment Situation in Maine - August
  2026\nSeptember 18\, 2026\nReleased: 10 a.m. Friday\, September 18\, 2026
 \nContact: Mark McInerney\, (207) 620-0197\nThe 3.2 unemployment rate in A
 ugust changed little from July. Nonfarm wage and salary jobs have remained
  within a narrow range for 28 consecutive months.\nSeasonally Adjusted Sta
 tewide Labor Force Estimates\nThe 3.2 percent unemployment rate changed li
 ttle from recent months. Unemployment rates have remained within a narrow 
 range between 3.1 and 3.3 percent for 24 consecutive months. Unemployment 
 has been below 4 percent for 56 months\, the longest duration on record. M
 aine's unemployment rate has been below the U.S. average for more than 18 
 years\, and remains below the long-term average of 5.4 percent for the sta
 te since 1976. Unemployment averaged 4.1 percent for New England and 4.1 p
 ercent for the U.S. in the month.\nThe labor force participation rate aver
 aged 58.8 percent over the last three months\, lower than during the three
  months through May. A decreasing employment-to-population ratio in the th
 ree months through August is the primary reason for lower rates of labor f
 orce participation.\nSeasonally Adjusted Statewide Nonfarm Jobs Estimates\
 nPreliminary seasonally adjusted estimates indicate that in August\, nonfa
 rm wage and salary jobs were little changed from July. Employment levels w
 ere stable in every sector.\nJob estimates have stayed within a narrow ran
 ge near 660\,000 for 28 consecutive months (estimates ranging less than 0.
 5 \nhttp://www.maine.gov/labor/news_events/article.shtml?id=13362371
